%S A399559 1,3,31,73,20581,237,59466793,324889,534746827,1184711,
%T A399559 7446124411999219,3488773,222324602061106592737,26924423821,
%U A399559 178558832774941,426100345038257,821678027107419201261226083683,270379773471,90335536134303965985948107659157953
%N A399559 Norm from Q(zeta_n) to Q of F(zeta_n), where F(q) = Sum_{k>=0} (q;q)_k is the Kontsevich-Zagier series and zeta_n is a primitive n-th root of unity.
%C A399559 Here (q;q)_k = Product_{j=1..k} (1-q^j), with (q;q)_0 = 1. If zeta_n is a primitive n-th root of unity, then (zeta_n;zeta_n)_k = 0 for k >= n, so the Kontsevich-Zagier series terminates and F(zeta_n) = Sum_{k=0..n-1} Product_{j=1..k} (1-zeta_n^j) is a cyclotomic integer. The sequence gives the norm of this algebraic integer from Q(zeta_n) to Q.
%C A399559 The same strange q-series is related to the Fishburn numbers A022493 through the substitution q = 1-x: the generating series for A022493 is the formal power series F(1-x). Thus A022493 records the coefficients obtained from F(1-x), while this sequence records an arithmetic invariant of the values of F at roots of unity.
%C A399559 If n = p^r, where p is prime and r >= 1, then a(n) == 1 (mod p).
%C A399559 For n > 2, the embeddings of Q(zeta_n) occur in complex-conjugate pairs. Their contributions to the norm therefore occur as sigma(F(zeta_n))*conj(sigma(F(zeta_n))) = |sigma(F(zeta_n))|^2, so a(n) is nonnegative. The cases n = 1 and n = 2 give 1 and 3 respectively.
%D A399559 C. Bijaoui, H. U. Boden, B. Myers, R. Osburn, W. Rushworth, A. Tronsgard and S. Zhou, Generalized Fishburn numbers and torus knots, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A 178 (2021), 105355.
%H A399559 C. Bijaoui, H. U. Boden, B. Myers, R. Osburn, W. Rushworth, A. Tronsgard and S. Zhou, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.00635">Generalized Fishburn numbers and torus knots</a>, arXiv:2002.00635 [math.NT], 2020.
%H A399559 D. Zagier, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0040-9383(00)00005-7">Vassiliev invariants and a strange identity related to the Dedekind eta-function</a>, Topology 40 (2001), 945-960.
%F A399559 Let F_n(x) = Sum_{k=0..n-1} Product_{j=1..k} (1-x^j).
%F A399559 a(n) = Norm_{Q(zeta_n)/Q}(F_n(zeta_n)) = Resultant(Phi(n,x), F_n(x)), where Phi(n,x) is the n-th cyclotomic polynomial.
%F A399559 Equivalently, a(n) = Product_{1 <= j <= n, gcd(j,n)=1} F_n(zeta_n^j).
%F A399559 If n = p^r, where p is prime and r >= 1, then a(n) == 1 (mod p).
%F A399559 Indeed, let P = (1-zeta_n). For n = p^r, P is the unique prime ideal above p and P intersect Z = (p). If gcd(j,n) = 1, then zeta_n^j == 1 (mod P), so F_n(zeta_n^j) == 1 (mod P). Taking the product over all Galois conjugates gives a(n) == 1 (mod P).
%F A399559 Since a(n) is an integer and P intersect Z = (p), it follows that a(n) == 1 (mod p).
%e A399559 For n = 3, F(zeta_3) = 1 + (1-zeta_3) + (1-zeta_3)(1-zeta_3^2) = 5-zeta_3, a(3) = Norm_{Q(zeta_3)/Q}(5-zeta_3) = (5-zeta_3)(5-zeta_3^2) = 31.
%e A399559 For n = 5, the corresponding norm is a(5) = 20581.
%o A399559 (SageMath)
%o A399559 R.<x> = PolynomialRing(ZZ)
%o A399559 def a(n):
%o A399559     Phi = R(cyclotomic_polynomial(n))
%o A399559     s = R(1)
%o A399559     p = R(1)
%o A399559     for k in range(1, n):
%o A399559         p *= 1 - x^k
%o A399559         s += p
%o A399559     return ZZ(Phi.resultant(s))
%o A399559 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 20)])
%o A399559 (PARI)
%o A399559 a(n)={my(s=1,p=1);if(n>1,for(k=1,n-1,p*=1-x^k;s+=p));polresultant(polcyclo(n),s)};
%o A399559 print(vector(19,n,a(n)));
%Y A399559 Cf. A022493 (Fishburn numbers), whose generating series is F(1-x), where F(q) is the Kontsevich-Zagier series used here.
%K A399559 nonn,new
%O A399559 1,2
